Goal
Build a playable 2D origami puzzle-platformer called Unfolded. This build contains only Level 1, "Blank Sheet": a tutorial with no enemies, made of a movement lane and three puzzle rooms (fold, flat form, wind). Priority order: game feel, readable puzzles, clean data-driven code. Visual polish comes after.
Tutorial design principles
Level 1 is a tutorial. Its job is to make a first-time player understand every mechanic without reading instructions, so build every space to teach. Follow these rules:
1. One new idea per space. Each section introduces exactly one mechanic, in a safe place, before anything is combined.
2. Teach in four beats: introduce it where it is obvious, let the player do it once, give one slightly harder use, then move on. For example, jump goes from a 1-tile step to a 2-tile block, and flat form gets a second, shorter slit.
3. Show, don't lecture. Prompts are short paper tags (4 words or fewer plus a drawn key cap). They appear only near the spot where they apply, fade once the player has done the action, and never pause the game or fill the screen with text.
4. The level explains itself. Interactive objects pulse softly, a dotted line links each switch to what it changes, and the geometry points to the answer (a ledge that is too high, with folded stairs beside it).
5. Safe to experiment. No enemies, no death, shallow pits, and instant reset with Backspace. A failure should show the player why it failed.
6. No skipping the lesson. Each gap, ledge and slit can only be passed with the mechanic being taught.
7. Gentle order: run, jump, higher jump, dash, fold switch, flat form, wind. Later rooms reuse earlier skills (the wind room also needs steering and the dash).
8. Hints, not answers. If the player fails the same spot 3 times, that spot's prompt returns with a soft pulse. Never solve it for them.
9. A payoff at the end. The sword pieces reward the player for having used every control.
Target: a first-time player finishes Level 1 with no outside help in about 5 minutes.
Stack and rules
• Lovable React + TypeScript + Vite project. Install Phaser 3 and mount a Phaser.Game inside a React component. Single page, no backend, no database, no login.
• No image or audio files. All art is drawn in code with Phaser Graphics (triangles and polygons with a thin dark outline). All sound is synthesised with the Web Audio API.
• Game resolution 960×540, scale to fit the window (Phaser Scale.FIT, centred). 1 tile = 32 px.
• Arcade physics for collisions, but the player controller is custom code (velocity, timers and states written by hand, not Arcade defaults).
• Put every tuning number in one file, src/game/constants.ts, and the level in a data file, src/game/levels/level1.ts (entity list, see "Level data"). Components for Switch, FoldPlatform, Fan, Slit, Checkpoint, Pickup, ExitDoor must be reusable classes configured by data, not hard-coded per room.
• Keep code in small files: Player.ts, Input.ts, Camera.ts, Audio.ts, Particles.ts, Level.ts, one file per component.
